11. Interview Explanation (30–60 sec)

> "The path score is the minimum edge on that path, and we want to maximize this minimum value. This suggests Binary Search on the answer. For a candidate score X, we discard all edges with cost less than X and check whether there is a valid path from source to destination with total cost at most k. Since the graph is a DAG, we compute the shortest path in linear time using topological order instead of Dijkstra. If the shortest valid path satisfies the budget, X is feasible, so we search higher; otherwise, we search lower. The final complexity is O((V + E) log C)."




---

12. Key Takeaways

DAG shortest path can be solved in O(V + E).

"Maximize the minimum" often implies Binary Search on Answer.

Binary search requires a monotonic feasibility condition.

Combining binary search with DAG DP yields an efficient solution.



---

13. Common Mistakes

Forgetting to skip offline intermediate nodes.

Allowing edges with cost < candidate.

Using BFS instead of shortest-path DP.

Forgetting to check dist[node] != INF before relaxing edges.

Modifying the original indegree array after topological sort without making a copy if reused.

Using Dijkstra unnecessarily on a DAG.



